To make the circle element in your CSS code appear more 3D, you can add some additional CSS properties that will create a depth effect. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` selector:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8);
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  transform: perspective(800px) rotateY(-45deg) translateZ(100px);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

Let me explain what each new property does:

1. `filter: brightness(0.8)` - This will slightly darken the color of the circle, making it appear less bright and more three-dimensional. You can adjust the value of the `brightness()` function to achieve the desired level of darkness.

2. `transform-style: preserve-3d` - This is necessary for creating the 3D effect because it tells the browser to treat this element as a three-dimensional object instead of just a flat surface.

3. `transform: perspective(800px) rotateY(-45deg) translateZ(100px)` - These are the actual properties that create the 3D effect. The `perspective()` function sets the distance between the viewer and the "camera" that's looking at the object. A higher value creates a deeper perspective. `rotateY()` rotates the object around the Y axis (which is perpendicular to the screen), giving it a tilted appearance. Finally, `translateZ()` moves the object forward along the Z axis (which is coming out of the screen). Again, you can adjust these values to achieve the desired effect.

