Here's the Laravel database migration to create a `users` table with foreign keys for many-to-many relationships with `cars` and `pets`, as well as a one-to-one relationship with `houses`:

```php
Schema::create('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->id();
    $table->string('name');
    $table->string('email')->unique();
    $table->timestamp('email_verified_at')->nullable();
    $table->string('password');
    $table->rememberToken();
    $table->timestamps();

    // Many-to-many relationships with cars and pets
    $table->unsignedBigInteger('car_ids')->index()->nullable();
    $table->foreign('car_ids')
        ->references('id')->on('cars')->onDelete('set null');

    $table->unsignedBigInteger('pet_ids')->index()->nullable();
    $table->foreign('pet_ids')
        ->references('id')->on('pets')->onDelete('set null');

    // One-to-one relationship with houses
    $table->unsignedBigInteger('house_id')->nullable();
    $table->foreign('house_id')
        ->references('id')->on('houses')->onDelete('set null');
});
```

In this example, we create the `users` table with fields for the user's name, email address, password, remember token, created_at and updated_at timestamps, and email verification timestamp. We also add two columns for storing the IDs of related `cars` and `pets` using the `unsignedBigInteger` type and the `index()` method to speed up queries. These columns have foreign key constraints referencing the corresponding tables' primary keys, and `onDelete('set null')` is specified to automatically clear out these references if a user is deleted.

Lastly, we add a column for the user's house using the same approach with a `nullable()` option so that it can be left empty if the user doesn't have a house yet. This column also has a foreign key constraint referencing the `houses` table's primary key.

